virglrenderer: video decoding on the Mac's media engine (VideoToolbox)

What: a VideoToolbox backend for virglrenderer's video protocol on macOS
(src/vrend/virgl_video_vt.c, built instead of the VA-API one). The guest's
VA-API driver (Mesa virgl) decodes H.264, HEVC Main and Main 10, VP9 profile 0
and 2 and AV1 (only with OMACVM_VIDEO_AV1=1) through VTDecompressionSession;
vrend_video.c copies each decoded IOSurface into the guest's plane textures
(GPU blit, CPU copy as fallback).
Guest limits (the guest is untrusted): decoders per VM are limited (each
holds a session on the Mac's media engine, shared with the Mac's own apps,
and its pictures). Each decoder's video caps tell the guest 32 (virgl_hw.h:
omacvm_max_open, 8 of the 20 reserved bits; Mesa ignores them), so OmacVM's
VA-API shim (src/app/guest/omacvm_drv_video.c) refuses vaCreateContext past
it and the player decodes on the CPU instead of showing a black video. The
host refuses only past 48: Mesa sends a decoder's close with the app's next
commands (the shim has freed the slot already), and apps outside the desktop
session do not use the shim. Past 48 one more gets none and decodes nothing;
closing one frees its slot, and a guest context that goes away frees its
own. A refusal is logged at once, then at most every 10 s with a count;
pictures for a refused decoder are dropped quietly, unknown handles are
logged the same way.
Compressed data is clamped to the guest's buffer and capped at 64 MiB per
picture. The GPU copy runs with the guest's conditional rendering paused
(vrend_renderer.c exports the pause vrend's own blits use) and rasterizer
discard off: either could drop the blit without a GL error and leave the
guest's old picture.
Why: video in the VM decodes on the media engine instead of the VM's CPU
(numbers in docs/video-decode.md).
Upstream: not submitted (docs/video-decode.md, "UTM", lists what it needs).
Tests: app/runtime/Tests/virgl/test-video-decode.c at build time (Apple's
software OpenGL; H.264 pictures land in the guest's planes, also under the
guest's conditional rendering and rasterizer discard, every decoder cap says
32, the host's 48-decoder limit, a closed context freeing its decoders). In a
VM: src/bench/video-bench.py and the checks in docs/video-decode.md.
