+/**
+ * @file
+ * VideoToolbox backend for virgl video (macOS hosts).
+ *
+ * The guest's VA-API driver (Mesa virgl) is a "stateless" decoder: it parses
+ * the stream itself and sends, per picture, the parsed parameters plus the
+ * compressed slice data. VideoToolbox is a "stateful" decoder: it wants the
+ * stream's parameter sets in a format description and whole access units as
+ * samples, and keeps its own reference frames.
+ *
+ * So this backend rebuilds a stream VideoToolbox can take:
+ *  - H.264: the slice NAL units come through unchanged; SPS and PPS are
+ *    rebuilt from the picture parameters (or taken from the data when the
+ *    guest sends them in-band).
+ *  - VP9: each frame comes through whole (uncompressed header included)
+ *    and goes to VideoToolbox as it is; hidden frames get their picture too.
+ *  - AV1 (opt-in, OMACVM_VIDEO_AV1=1): Chrome sends the whole temporal unit
+ *    for every frame in it; the frame's own OBUs are cut out and decoded
+ *    alone. FFmpeg sends tile data without headers, which cannot work.
+ * Reference frames are tracked by VideoToolbox from the bitstream, so the
+ * guest's reference surface ids are not needed.
+ *
+ * Decoded pictures come back as CVPixelBuffers (NV12 or P010), and
+ * vrend_video.c copies their planes into the guest's plane textures.
+ *
+ * Profile numbers: the guest sends Mesa's enum pipe_video_profile values.
+ * Mesa 26.0 removed the MPEG-4 part 2 profiles and Mesa 24 added more HEVC
+ * profiles, so a current guest and virglrenderer's own copy of the enum no
+ * longer agree. The guest numbering used here is Mesa's current one
+ * (OMACVM_VIRGL_VIDEO_ABI=legacy switches to virglrenderer's).
+ */
+
